  "accounting": "Adopted TER 4 June 2026, Article 11.3, confirms 60 required EC each in years one and two and 30 required plus 30 open in year three. The annual Guide and research unit corroborate the 12 EC research and thesis allocation. Article 9.1 sets the criminal-law and research prerequisites. The ordinary Dutch pathway has no formal specialization. Article 15.2 adjustments concern earlier-regulation students in named optional units. Dutch diagnostic and conditional refresher carry no additional EC. The optional forensic minor remains outside the required course count.",
  "progression": "The adopted 2026–2027 regulations, Articles 7.1, 9.1, 10.2 and 11.3, establish the general Dutch-language bachelor. Years one and two each contain ten required 6 EC courses. Year three includes three 6 EC courses, the 12 EC research unit and 30 EC of open study. Principles of Criminal Law precedes substantive and procedural criminal law. The ordinary research route requires quantitative and qualitative criminological methods and combines a group investigation with an individual thesis. The current module describes a conditional enrolment process for students completing qualitative methods in the same academic year. It does not alter the ordinary progression. The Dutch language test and any required refresher are conditions for the first-year project grade without separate credits. The forensic minor is optional.",
